    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey, i'm working on a deck build in vancouver. using western red cedar. can u explain the main difference between live load and dead load for the sizing calcs? need to make sure my post spacing is right.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Dead load refers to the permanent, fixed weight of your deck materials, like the wood itself, and should be calculated precisely based on material densities. Live load accounts for temporary, variable weight, such as people, snow accumulation, or furniture on the deck. When sizing posts, you must ensure they can safely handle the combined total of both the dead load and the maximum expected live load specific to Vancouver's climate and intended use.",
      "source": "target",
